ed on 2026-06-03 is exactly 7 days ago (or slightly less depending on time), so \"more than 7 days ago\" means created_at < 2026-06-03. String comparison works for ISO dates.\n\nActually, let me be careful: if created_at is \"2026-06-03T10:00:00Z\" and today is 2026-06-10 (unknown time), then it's roughly 7 days ago. More than 7 days would require before 2026-06-03T00:00:00. Using date string comparison \"2026-06-03\" as cutoff: \"2026-06-02T...\" < \"2026-06-03\" is true. \"2026-06-03T00:00:00Z\" < \"2026-06-03\" is false (since \"2026-06-03T...\" > \"2026-06-03\" because it's longer with same prefix). So cutoff = \"2026-06-03\" with string less-than gives created on or before 2026-06-02. That matches \"more than 7 days ago\" reasonably.\n\nHmm, but edge case: a ticket created \"2026-06-03T00:00:00\" exactly — that's exactly 7 days ago if now is 2026-06-10T00:00:00. \"More than 7 days\" excludes it. Fine.\n\nAlso \"mentions a refund\" — body mentions refund. Check for \"refund\" case-insensitively. That covers \"refund\", \"Refund\", \"refunded\", \"refunding\". Good.\n\nLet me run the compose script. Then submit_result with the IDs.\n\nOne concern: does update_ticket use \"add_tags\" or \"tags\"? The tool schema shows properties: id, priority, add_tags. The description says \"append tags\". So use add_tags. The compose docs said update_ticket: { id?, priority?, tags? } but the actual tool schema says add_tags. I'll use add_tags as per the real schema.\n\nLet me run it.",
